// @test regression/INV160-tuple-literal-positions
// @description Where a tuple literal is legal, cell by cell. The discriminator is TOP LEVEL vs INSIDE A BLOCK plus value position - NOT final vs non-final, which is what the machinery in both this checker and piners was built around. TV takes a bare tuple statement anywhere inside a block including a `for` body (never a return position), rejects one at top level even as the last statement, and rejects every value position. Its two messages differ and anchor differently: the top-level one at the END of the tuple (TV parses it as a destructuring target and demands `=`), the value-position one at the BRACKET. The legal cells matter as much as the errors - a fix keyed on "a bracket at statement start" would break the destructuring at the bottom, which is how the legal form begins. Ternary BRANCHES are excluded on purpose: those are CE10163 and belong to INV127. NOTE `--tv` on THIS file reports only the first error and then stops (G001), so it confirms the legal cells above it and nothing below; each error cell is adjudicated individually by the isolated probes in investigations/INV160-tuple-literal-positions/probes/, all `--tv` clean-diffed 2026-08-25. See INV160, TODO #75.
// @expects parse: success
// @expects errors: 3
// @expects error: line=24, message=/expecting set "="/
// @expects error: line=26, message=/Syntax error at input "\["/
// @expects error: line=27, message=/Syntax error at input "\["/
//@version=6
indicator("tuple literal positions")
a = 1
b = 2

// LEGAL: anywhere inside a block, final or not, and a `for` body is never a
// return position.
f() =>
    for i = 0 to 1
        [a, b]
        a + b
    [a, b]

g() =>
    switch
        close > open => [a, b]
        => [b, a]

if close > open
    [a, b]
    a + b

// ERROR: top level, even though this one is not the last statement.
[a, b]

c = [a, b] == [a, b]
d = not [a, b]

// LEGAL: the destructuring, which begins exactly like the error above and
// must stay clean.
[x, y] = f()
[p, q] = g()
plot(x + y + p + q + (c ? 1 : 0) + (d ? 1 : 0))
